\chapter{The global host record}
Every runtime creates an immutable global binding named \api{G}. Its members
provide the minimal bridge needed by the language and its packages. A local
scope may shadow the name, but ordinary package evaluation receives the
runtime's original global record.

\begin{description}
\item[LoadLib(name)] Discover and evaluate a package; return a record or nil.
\item[Native(name)] Retrieve a host-registered native value; unknown names throw.
\item[GetStdout()] Return a callable sink accepting one string.
\item[DieWith(value)] Throw an error containing the value's display text.
\item[Assert(value)] Require a true value and return it.
\item[Str(value)] Convert a value to display text.
\item[Type(value)] Return the kind name.
\item[Len(value)] Count string bytes, list elements, or record entries.
\end{description}

\begin{mullcode}
val output <- G:GetStdout();
output("direct sink\n");
G:Assert(G:Len({a: 1, b: 2}) == 2);
G:Assert(G:Str([1,2]) == "[1, 2]");
\end{mullcode}

The sink returned by \api{GetStdout} follows the runtime's current
\api{stdoutSink} callback. Replacing that host callback therefore redirects
existing stdout sink values as well as newly obtained ones. A script may
instead install its own sink on the formatting library.

Display conversion is intended for messages, not serialization. Strings are
displayed without surrounding quotes, and functions display as
\api{<function>}. Numbers use 17 significant digits to preserve binary64
round trips. A durable data format should be provided by a separate codec.
